---
import Learn from '../../layouts/Learn.astro';
import MemoryFlow from '../../components/learn/MemoryFlow.astro';
import BarChart from '../../components/learn/BarChart.astro';
import { COMPANY, PRICES, TRIAL } from '../../data/site';

// Learn comparison page 7 (s94). Rules: see ai-memory-apps-compared.astro and
// data/pricing.ts. Facts read on 2026-10-13:
// - Obsidian pricing (obsidian.md/pricing): personal use "Free without limits";
//   Sync $4 a month billed yearly ($48 a year) or $5 monthly, "AES-256
//   end-to-end encryption, preventing us from reading it"; commercial licence
//   optional, $50 a year.
// - Basic Memory (basicmemory.com, docs): local $0, open source, plain Markdown,
//   works with Obsidian; Python 3.12+; Cloud from $15 a month.
// - Obsidian has no built-in MCP server: AI apps reach a vault through community
//   plugins or a tool like Basic Memory (Obsidian's own pages describe no AI
//   connection; research s94).
const CHECKED = '13 October 2026';
const OUTLINE = [
  { id: 'short', title: 'The short answer' },
  { id: 'obsidian', title: 'Obsidian as AI memory' },
  { id: 'app', title: 'A memory app' },
  { id: 'cost', title: 'What a year costs' },
  { id: 'both', title: 'Using both' },
];
const OBSIDIAN = 'https://obsidian.md/pricing';
const BASIC = 'https://basicmemory.com/';
---
<Learn
  path="/learn/obsidian-vs-ai-memory-app/"
  lede="Obsidian is a notes app, and a very good one: your notes are files on your computer that you write and read. A memory app is written to by your AI apps as you talk to them, and searched by them later. Use Obsidian when you want to write the knowledge yourself; use a memory app when you want your AI apps to remember you without you keeping notes."
  outline={OUTLINE}
>
  <section id="short">
    <h2>The short answer</h2>
    <p>People use Obsidian as AI memory in two ways: pasting notes into a chat, or connecting the
       vault to an AI app through a community plugin or a tool like Basic Memory. It works, and many
       people like it, because every memory is a note they can open and fix. The trade is that you
       look after the notes, and connecting AI apps to them takes some setup.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="obsidian">
    <h2>Obsidian as AI memory</h2>
    <p>Obsidian is free for personal use, "Free without limits" in its own words. Sync between
       devices costs $4 a month billed yearly, and Obsidian says synced notes are protected with
       AES-256 end-to-end encryption, "preventing us from reading it"
       (<a href={OBSIDIAN}>Obsidian's pricing</a>). It runs on phones too.</p>
    <p>Obsidian itself doesn't connect to AI apps. For Claude or Cursor to read and write your
       vault you add a community plugin, or a separate tool such as Basic Memory, which is free, open
       source and writes plain Markdown that Obsidian can open (<a href={BASIC}>Basic Memory's
       site</a>). That tool needs Python and some setup.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="app">
    <h2>A memory app</h2>
    <p>A memory app is built for the AI to use. Your apps save short facts as you talk, like a
       preference, a deadline or a person's name, and look them up later without being asked to
       open a note. Zaaheen, which we make, keeps that memory encrypted on your computer and shares
       it between Claude Desktop, Claude Code, Cursor, the ChatGPT app, Codex and other apps. You
       can see and delete your memories in the app, but you don't write them as notes.</p>

    <MemoryFlow
      id="flow-obsidian"
      from="ChatGPT"
      to="Claude Code"
      fact="Project deadline: 30 Nov"
      others={['Cursor']}
      caption="A memory app saves a fact from one chat on its own, with no note to write, and your other AI apps find it there later."
    />

    <table class="learn-table">
      <thead>
        <tr><th scope="col"></th><th scope="col">Obsidian (with an AI plugin)</th><th scope="col">Zaaheen</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><th scope="row">Who writes the memory</th><td>Mostly you; the AI can add notes through a plugin</td><td>Your AI apps, as you talk</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">Can you read it as notes</th><td>Yes, every note is a file</td><td>You see and delete memories in the app; not as files</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">AI apps</th><td>Through community plugins or tools like Basic Memory</td><td>Built in, from the Agents tab</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">Encryption</th><td>End-to-end on Obsidian Sync</td><td>On your computer</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">Phones</th><td>Yes</td><td>No</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p class="learn-note">As of {CHECKED}, from Obsidian's and Basic Memory's own websites, and may
       change. Check the links above for the latest.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="cost">
    <h2>What a year costs</h2>
    <BarChart
      id="chart-obsidian-year"
      title="One year for one person, in US dollars"
      measure="US dollars a year"
      max={60}
      bars={[
        { label: 'Obsidian', value: 0, note: 'free for personal use' },
        { label: 'Basic Memory (local)', value: 0, note: 'free' },
        { label: 'Obsidian with Sync', value: 48, note: '$4 a month, yearly' },
        { label: 'Zaaheen', value: PRICES.yearly, note: 'yearly plan' },
      ]}
      source={{ text: `Each company's own website and ours, prices as of ${CHECKED} and may change.` }}
    />
  </section>

  <section id="both">
    <h2>Using both</h2>
    <p>They don't compete as much as it looks. Plenty of people keep their notes and documents in
       Obsidian and let a memory app handle the small, personal facts their AI apps keep asking for.
       If you love writing notes and want them on your phone, Obsidian alone may be all you need. If
       you'd rather your AI apps just remembered you, try Zaaheen free for {TRIAL.days} days.</p>
    <p>Where Zaaheen falls short: no phone version; Windows and Macs with Apple chips only; one
       computer, with no sync yet; your memories aren't files you can edit by hand.</p>
    <p class="learn-note">Obsidian and Basic Memory are names of their owners. We have no link with
       either. Facts checked on {CHECKED}; if anything here is wrong or out of date, tell us at
       <a href={`mailto:${COMPANY.email}`}>{COMPANY.email}</a> and we'll correct it.</p>
  </section>
</Learn>
